5, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": 0.2}, "rating_margin": 6.1, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.15, "baseline_margin": 5.53, "analyst_adjustment": -0.53, "key_factors": ["Strong Democratic-leaning expert consensus (9 of 10 raters call it a D flip)", "Prediction markets price Luria around 91% to win", "October CNU poll shows Luria up 3 among likely voters", "Kiggans faces heavy outside opposition spending ($2.7M against her)"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic-leaning environment in an even-PVI district, and nearly every expert rater plus both major prediction markets now favor Luria, with the lone October poll showing a 3-point lead. The thin and partly dated polling keeps uncertainty elevated, but the weight of evidence justifies moving slightly beyond a pure Lean D call.", "watch": "A late surge in Republican-leaning early voting or a fresh poll showing Kiggans tied or ahead would move this back toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jen Kiggans (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 2nd District is a rematch between incumbent Republican Jen Kiggans and former Democratic Representative Elaine Luria in a Navy-heavy, politically even seat. The district carries no partisan lean on paper, but the 2026 national environment appears favorable to Democrats, and Luria has kept pace with Kiggans financially while outside groups have spent heavily against the incumbent.\n\nA Christopher Newport University poll in mid-October showed Luria ahead by three points among likely voters, and nine of ten expert raters now call the race a Democratic flip, with several rating it Likely D. Prediction markets price Luria's chances around 91 percent. The main caution is that polling here is sparse, so the race remains closer than the market suggests, but the overall evidence points to a modest Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7963, "margin": 4.98}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8168, "margin": 5.5}, {"date